.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt TERM 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm term
.Nd terminal on the whole framebuffer
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl l | Fl H
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Ar program Op Ar arg ...
.Nm
.Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a terminal that runs in user space on the whole framebuffer.
It runs
.Ar program ,
found on
.Ev PATH ,
or with no
.Ar program
the shell
.Ev SHELL
names,
.Pa /bin/sh
when it is unset, on a pty the size of the screen, with
.Ev TERM
set to
.Ql xterm-256color
and
.Ev COLORTERM
to
.Ql truecolor .
It parses what the program writes with libtsm, draws the cells with GNU
Unifont in 24-bit color, and gives the program the keys of the console's
keymap as xterm sends them, or by the Kitty keyboard protocol when the
program asks for it.
It answers the probes programs send: DA1, the cursor position, XTVERSION,
DECRQM, the Kitty query, and OSC 10 and 11.
.Pp
.Nm
takes the screen by opening
.Pa /fb
and the keyboard by putting
.Pa /dev/console
in K_MEDIUMRAW and raw mode, and gives both back when the program exits.
A program it starts may take the screen in turn, as Doom does; see
.Xr console 4 .
The kernel console stays beneath it, with
.Ql TERM=rach2 ,
and comes back when
.Nm
exits or is killed.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-c file"
.It Fl l
Run
.Pa /bin/login
in place of a program; no
.Ar program
is taken.
.Xr init 8
starts
.Nm
.Fl l
as the console session on a boot with
.Ql rach2.console=term .
.It Fl H
Run the program on a host pty with no screen, and answer commands read
from standard input, for tests.
.Pa term/Readme.md
in the source tree lists them.
.It Fl c Ar file
Read the settings from
.Ar file
in place of
.Pa /etc/term.conf ;
see
.Xr term.conf 5 .
.It Fl V
Print
.Ql libtsm
and its version.
.El
.Pp
Every key but these reaches the program:
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl+Shift+Page Up"
.It Shift+Page Up , Shift+Page Down
Show the scrollback a page further back or forward.
On the alternate screen, which keeps none, the program gets the keys.
.It Ctrl+Shift+Page Up , Ctrl+Shift+Page Down
Draw the font at 2x or at 1x, and keep the scale in the settings file.
.It Print Screen
Turn the selection mode on, and off again.
.It Shift+Print Screen
Paste the clipboard.
.El
.Pp
In the selection mode the cursor keys and
.Ql h ,
.Ql j ,
.Ql k
and
.Ql l
move its cursor, Space or
.Ql v
marks the start, Enter or
.Ql y
copies the cells from the mark to the cursor and leaves, and Escape or
.Ql q
leaves.
A selection and OSC 52 replace the one clipboard, which is emptied when
.Nm
exits.
A paste sends it with each LF as CR and with ESC left out, bracketed
under mode 2004.
.Pp
The bell, BEL, plays a soft chime of 880 Hz through
.Pa /dev/dsp ,
mixed over what other programs play there, or shows the screen in
inverse colors, or both or neither, as the settings'
.Va bell
says.
A bell while the chime plays is dropped.
When
.Pa /dev/dsp
cannot be opened, as on a machine whose only sound is the PC speaker,
the bell is a short soft beep on the speaker through
.Pa /dev/console ;
a program sets the beep's pitch and length with Linux's
.Ql "ESC [ 10 ; N ]"
and
.Ql "ESC [ 11 ; N ]" ,
and a length of 0 silences the chime as well; see
.Xr term.conf 5 .
.Pp
.Xr showkey 1
is run on the kernel console, since under
.Nm
the two would read the same keys.
.Xr setleds 1
and
.Xr kbdrate 8
act on the console that is their standard input, so under
.Nm
they are run with
.Ql < /dev/console .
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width SHELL
.It Ev SHELL
The shell run when no
.Ar program
is given.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/share/term/unifont.font
.It Pa /etc/term.conf
The settings.
.It Pa /usr/share/term/unifont.font
The packed font.
.It Pa /fb
The framebuffer.
.It Pa /dev/console
The keyboard and the speaker.
.It Pa /dev/dsp
The chime.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with the program's status, or 128 and the number of the signal that
ended it; with
.Fl l ,
with 0 once the login session has ended, however it ended.
It exits with 1 when it cannot start, or when SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGINT or
SIGQUIT ends it, which hangs the program up, and with 2 for a wrong usage.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr console 4 ,
.Xr dsp 4 ,
.Xr term.conf 5 ,
.Xr init 8
